Summary — Agentic Exception Investigation & Continuous Improvement

MVP: This innovation proposes testing a controlled agentic investigation pattern against a single existing deterministic automation. When the automation encounters an unknown condition or failure it cannot resolve, it would generate a standardized investigation package containing the relevant timestamp, inputs, outputs/errors, logs, application context, and other available evidence. That package would trigger a short-lived agent session with narrowly scoped, primarily read-only access to the resources required for investigation, such as the relevant source code and logs. Guided by an investigation skill that defines required evidence, permitted actions, and termination conditions, the agent would investigate the exception and produce a structured finding containing its evidence, likely cause, and proposed remediation. The result could then be captured in Jira for engineer review, after which the agent session terminates. The MVP would be evaluated against real historical or controlled exceptions to determine diagnosis quality, usefulness of proposed solutions, investigation time, cost, and reliability compared with normal manual investigation.

Successful implementation and expansion: If the MVP demonstrates reliable value, the pattern could be abstracted into a reusable Agentic Exception Investigation Framework. Existing scripts, monitoring systems, or applications could emit a common investigation event when they encounter conditions requiring deeper analysis. The framework could progressively integrate approved sources such as application logs, GitHub repositories, Jira, monitoring platforms, BigPanda/outage information, documentation, and a history of previous investigations. Deterministic controls would define what information and tools each investigation may access, while the agent retains flexibility to investigate within those boundaries. Repeated discoveries could then become proposed enhancements to the underlying deterministic automation, creating a controlled improvement cycle: automation → unknown condition → agent investigation → evidence and proposed solution → human review → deterministic enhancement. Nightly or weekly processing could provide a cost-effective starting cadence for non-critical exceptions, with additional triggering models introduced only where the use case warrants them.

Value / Benefits: The goal is not to replace deterministic automation or engineering judgment with AI, but to apply agentic reasoning specifically where traditional automation becomes expensive: the unknown cases. Engineers currently spend time gathering logs, locating relevant code, correlating timestamps and monitoring events, reproducing failures, documenting findings, and creating remediation work. An agent that can perform portions of that discovery consistently and preserve its evidence could reduce investigation time while producing better-documented engineering work. Because routine cases remain deterministic and agent sessions are created only for exceptions, the approach also limits AI cost and exposure. Most importantly, successful investigations can reveal repeatable patterns that engineers convert into deterministic fixes, allowing the automation itself to become more capable over time and reserving future agent effort for genuinely new problems.